<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#manilla">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manilla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A manilla was a horseshoe-shaped bracelet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> or brass used as currency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Manillas were introduced in large quantities through European trade, especially by the Portuguese, and were used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, manillas were a medium of exchange and were melted down for the famous Benin bronzes.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of the metal for the Benin Bronzes came from Portuguese brass <b>manillas</b>, horseshoe-shaped bracelets used as currency in trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Five objects from Isaiah Anozie’s first finds are in the British Museum: a small staff, a ram’s head, a large <b>manilla</b>, a crescent-shaped vessel and the pendant of a chief’s head.</blockquote><a href="/culture/igbo-ukwu-culture">Igbo-Ukwu culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>By the lost-wax method they cast the commemorative heads for the royal altars and the plaques that covered the palace pillars, which record the court’s ranks in dress and emblems; much of their brass came from <b>manillas</b>, the brass rings that Portuguese traders brought from the Rhineland.</blockquote><a href="/societies/benin">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Benin</a></li></ul></section>
